{"id":"deferrals","ord":"a0","title":"Deferrals","desc":"NO DEFERRALS (the human, 2026-08-22): a PR ships everything its item names and everything it spots in touched code; what cannot be done STOPS and ASKS. The Inbox holds only what the human chooses to park. ONE exception: a test found flaky on master that a PR does not own is NOT a deferral — it goes to the Inbox's top-level `flaky tests` ledger, governed by dag-pr-ci's FLAKE POLICY.","created":"2026-08-21T17:25:18-04:00","changed":"2026-08-29T10:22:09-04:00"}
{"id":"flaky-tests","ord":"a7","title":"flaky tests — the ledger of tests found flaky on master that no PR owns","desc":"THE LEDGER, not the law: one entry per test file, sightings and dispositions as props on each entry. THE LAW lives in the boot chain — dag-pr-ci's FLAKE POLICY (filed here, dispatched immediately, pi or grok never Opus, one PR when several are open, auto-merge on green). The CI watcher (roster: claude-ci) is the reporting channel; the orchestrator files what it reports.","created":"2026-08-22T11:51:06-04:00","changed":"2026-08-29T10:21:35-04:00"}
{"id":"awaiting-word","ord":"a8","title":"Awaiting the human's word","desc":"The orchestrator's surfaced-but-unruled ledger, per orch-findings-inbox: each child names a proposal or observation and the word it awaits. Ruled children get their outcome written and are trashed or promoted to a lane.","created":"2026-08-29T11:40:15-04:00"}
{"id":"design-decisions","ord":"a9","title":"design decisions — agents' judgment calls awaiting the human's review","desc":"Choices agents made on their own, for your review — ONLY two kinds reach this queue (your ruling, 2026-08-29 20:15): (1) decisions with USER-FACING impact (what you see, how it behaves, where your data/config lives); (2) creation or non-creation of packages/*. Everything lower-level stays in the PR body's ## Design decisions section for reviewers and never lands here. One parent per PR, one child per qualifying choice, plain words. Reviewed: fine → trash; wrong → a lane to reverse it.","created":"2026-08-29T17:29:00-04:00","changed":"2026-08-29T20:17:08-04:00"}
{"id":"flake-deliveries","parent":"flaky-tests","ord":"a0","title":"packages/chat/src/deliveries.test.ts is flaky on master","todo":true,"desc":"Found in passing by #453's author and deliberately NOT fixed there (a fix smuggled into a packaging move is what the scope fence forbids). Their measurement: 9 runs on the fold's head gave 7 pass / 2 fail; 4 runs on master at 12b3b6ad gave 3 pass / 1 fail — same two cases, same stream error. So the flake predates the fold and no PR owns it, which is what puts it here.\n\n**The error string, from #454's author on 2026-09-01: `Controller is already closed`, under FULL-SUITE LOAD, green on re-run.** #455's author saw a one-off 4957/1 on a full run and lost the identity to a `tail -4`; three later full runs and six runs of the file alone were clean. CI's own `test` recipe has gone green at every head this week. So the shape is settled: **load-dependent, not random** — which is why it passes in isolation and in CI, and why a fix must be reproduced under load rather than by running the one file.\n\nWORTH THE HUMAN'S EYE BEYOND THE LEDGER: this is the DOORBELL's own delivery test, from #450 — the mechanism that wakes the orchestrator's conversation on fleet events. A flaky test over the waking path is the one flake that can hide a real fault in the thing that reports every other fault. So the lane's real question is not only \"why is the test flaky\" but \"is the test flaky, or is the delivery path racy under load\" — a controller closed twice can be a teardown racing a write, or a product that genuinely double-closes.\n\n**RULED BY THE HUMAN, 2026-09-01: grok authors, and this lane takes NO REVIEWER.** That makes CI the only gate between the author and master, which the brief states plainly to the author. Auto-merge on green, per the standing flake-class grant.\n\n**NOT YET DISPATCHED — the human called a hold at 09:52 after the brief was written.** Everything is prepared: brief, agent, tier, merge policy. Nothing is spawned and no step exists; the lane starts on their word.","created":"2026-08-31T21:49:39-04:00","changed":"2026-09-01T09:51:50-04:00","custom":{"agent":"agent-grok","brief":"briefs/flake-deliveries.md","merge":"merge-auto","node":"appliance-fold","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/453","tier":"mechanical"}}
{"id":"await-subagent-leak","parent":"awaiting-word","ord":"a0","title":"Async subagent results spill raw into the chat panel","todo":true,"desc":"Reported by the human 2026-09-01 ~16:00 and VERIFIED same hour from this conversation's own artifacts (their screenshot + the session transcript + olai's service log).\n\n**The verified chain:** (1) the harness's async Agent tool injects, at completion, a `user`-role turn whose content is ONE RAW STRING — the `<task-notification>` XML with the subagent's whole report inside — and stamps it `origin.kind: \"task-notification\"` (real human turns carry `origin.kind: \"human\"`; both read from the session JSONL, 2a3c4d88, line 1353). (2) olai's service log shows the three notifications arriving as ordinary prompts (`prompt sent`/`turn ended` at 19:00:23, 19:01:16, 19:05:19 UTC). (3) The panel drew the raw payload inline in the main column, markdown unrendered (screenshot).\n\n**The bug:** the discriminator exists in the session data and nothing downstream reads it — the adapter/panel treat a labeled harness-injection as ordinary conversation content. docs/chat.md's contract (subagent reports fold under the spawning row; a task's ending is one row in the harness's word) is broken twice in one frame.\n\n**The fix's home:** the pinned adapter, where the label is readable — classify `origin.kind: \"task-notification\"` turns and deliver them as the task-ending row + folded report; panel work only if a new chunk kind is needed. The repro is trivial (spawn an async agent from an olai chat).\n\nAwaiting the human's word to cut the lane.","created":"2026-09-01T15:52:42-04:00","changed":"2026-09-01T15:56:13-04:00"}
